(typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports ? data => { module.exports = data; } : data => registerLessonPack('en', data))({
  "dfir-01-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Operations Architecture</div>\n  <h1>SOC Architecture, SIEM &amp; Syslog Pipelines</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">The Security Operations Center (SOC) serves as the nerve center of enterprise defensive operations. In the face of billions of daily security events, how do teams achieve sub-second log ingestion, normalization, and automated correlation? This lesson dissects multi-tiered SOC operational workflows and high-throughput SIEM/Syslog pipelines.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The 3-Tier SOC Operational Framework</strong>: L1 alert triage, L2 incident escalation and containment, and L3 proactive threat hunting and malware reverse engineering.</li>\n    <li><strong>Syslog Architecture (RFC 5424)</strong>: Decoupling Facility/Severity priorities and building high-volume pipelines using Beats, Kafka, and Logstash.</li>\n    <li><strong>Authoring Multi-Event SIEM Correlation Rules</strong>: Constructing time-window queries using Splunk SPL and Elastic Query DSL.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The 3-Tier SOC Operational Structure</h2>\n<p>An enterprise SOC functions as a specialized triage and incident remediation pipeline:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Operational Tier</th>\n      <th>Mission &amp; Responsibilities</th>\n      <th>Core SLA Benchmark</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Tier 1: Triage Analyst</strong></td>\n      <td>Monitors SIEM dashboards, eliminates false positives, validates true-positive signals, and escalates confirmed breaches</td>\n      <td>Mean Time to Acknowledge (MTTA) &lt; 15 mins</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Tier 2: Incident Responder</strong></td>\n      <td>Assumes custody of compromised hosts, performs forensic dumps, contains malicious processes, and identifies root cause</td>\n      <td>Mean Time to Remediate (MTTR) &lt; 2 hours</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Tier 3: Threat Hunter</strong></td>\n      <td>Operates under the assumption of breach; mines historical telemetry for sophisticated APT campaigns and zero-days</td>\n      <td>Engineers novel detection rules &amp; behavioral models</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Syslog Architecture &amp; Log Ingestion Pipelines</h2>\n<p>Enterprise firewalls, Linux endpoints, and network appliances transmit event telemetry using the standard <strong>Syslog protocol (RFC 5424)</strong>:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Firewalls / Linux Endpoints] ──RFC 5424 (UDP/TCP 514)──▶ [Logstash / Fluentd]\n                                                                │\n                                                                ▼\n[Elasticsearch / OpenSearch] ◀──Kafka Message Bus── [Elastic Common Schema (ECS)]\n          │\n          ▼\n[SIEM Dashboards / Correlation Rules] ──Alert Trigger──▶ [Tier 1 Analyst Triage]</code></pre>\n\n<p>A standard RFC 5424 Syslog datagram follows this structure:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&lt;134&gt;1 2026-03-15T10:30:00.000Z firewall.corp.internal sshd 2410 - - Failed password for invalid user admin from 10.0.0.5 port 42812 ssh2</code></pre>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>&lt;134&gt;</code> Priority value (PRI): Computed as <code>Facility * 8 + Severity</code>. Here, 134 represents <code>Facility=16 (local0)</code> and <code>Severity=6 (Informational)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>3. Practical: Authoring Brute-Force Correlation Rules</h2>\n<p>A solitary failed authentication represents normal noise. However, multiple consecutive failures followed by a successful login within a 5-minute window indicates credential stuffing or brute-forcing:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-spl\"># Splunk SPL: Detecting successful brute-force attacks within a 5-minute window\nindex=security sourcetype=WinEventLog:Security (EventCode=4625 OR EventCode=4624)\n| eval Status=if(EventCode=4625, \"Failure\", \"Success\")\n| stats count(eval(Status=\"Failure\")) as FailCount, \n        count(eval(Status=\"Success\")) as SuccessCount, \n        earliest(_time) as FirstAttempt, \n        latest(_time) as LastAttempt \n        by Source_Network_Address, Target_User_Name\n| where FailCount &gt;= 10 AND SuccessCount &gt;= 1\n| eval TimeDiff = LastAttempt - FirstAttempt\n| where TimeDiff &lt; 300</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Modern SOC defense relies on a <strong>3-tier model: L1 triage, L2 incident remediation, and L3 threat hunting</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>The Syslog protocol and Elastic Common Schema (ECS)</strong> normalize heterogeneous enterprise event telemetry into a unified format.</li>\n    <li>The true power of SIEM engines lies in <strong>statistical time-window correlation across disparate log sources</strong> rather than isolated alerts.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Syslog Priority Calculation</div>\n  <p>Analyze the following Syslog header: <code>&lt;34&gt;1 2026-03-15T...</code></p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Given that <code>PRI = Facility * 8 + Severity</code>, calculate the exact Facility number and Severity level for <code>&lt;34&gt;</code>.</li>\n    <li>Does this severity represent a system Emergency or a Critical error?</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In a modern three-tier enterprise Security Operations Center (SOC), what is the primary operational responsibility of a Tier 1 (L1) analyst?","options":["Authoring kernel-level EDR sensor drivers and hardware firmware reverse engineering","24/7 high-volume alert monitoring, initial validation triage, false-positive elimination, and escalation","Interfacing directly with regulators and law enforcement for corporate public relations","Procuring and racking enterprise datacenter hardware servers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"L1 analysts operate on the telemetry frontline, monitoring continuous alert queues, filtering out benign noise, and rapidly escalating confirmed security incidents to Tier 2/3 investigators."},{"question":"According to RFC 5424 Syslog specifications, if a log packet possesses Facility code 4 (auth) and Severity code 1 (Alert), what is its computed PRI header value?","options":["33","41","5","32"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Syslog priority value is calculated as PRI = Facility * 8 + Severity. Therefore, 4 * 8 + 1 = 33."}]},
  "dfir-01-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Log Forensics</div>\n  <h1>Windows Security Event Logs: 4624, 4672 &amp; 7045</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Windows endpoints and Active Directory domain controllers form the backbone of corporate enterprise infrastructure. Regardless of stealth techniques, adversary authentication, privilege assignment, and service creation inevitably leave indelible cryptographic event traces. This lesson analyzes Windows Security Event IDs 4624, 4625, 4672, and 7045.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The Logon Type Matrix of Event 4624</strong>: Distinguishing Type 2 (Interactive keyboard), Type 3 (Network SMB/IPC), and Type 10 (Remote Desktop RDP).</li>\n    <li><strong>Indicators of Lateral Movement</strong>: Tracking Event 4672 (Privilege Assignment), Event 4720 (Backdoor Account Creation), and Event 7045 (PsExec Service Installation).</li>\n    <li><strong>Batch Telemetry Queries with PowerShell</strong>: Harvesting high-fidelity lateral movement timelines from millions of EVTX records in milliseconds.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Deciphering Event ID 4624: The Logon Type Field</h2>\n<p>Whenever a session authenticates on Windows, the kernel generates <strong>Event ID 4624 (Audit Success)</strong>. The primary field that separates benign user activity from adversary lateral movement is the <strong>Logon Type</strong>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Logon Type</th>\n      <th>Designation</th>\n      <th>Operational Context &amp; Attack Scenarios</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2</strong></td>\n      <td>Interactive</td>\n      <td>Physical console logon via local keyboard/display</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3</strong></td>\n      <td>Network</td>\n      <td><strong>High-frequency lateral movement indicator!</strong> Authenticating over SMB, IPC$ shares, or via tools like CrackMapExec</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4</strong></td>\n      <td>Batch</td>\n      <td>Invoked by Task Scheduler running under specified user credentials</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>5</strong></td>\n      <td>Service</td>\n      <td>Generated when a background Windows service launches</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>7</strong></td>\n      <td>Unlock</td>\n      <td>User unlocks an existing workstation session</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>10</strong></td>\n      <td>RemoteInteractive</td>\n      <td><strong>Remote Desktop (RDP) connection!</strong> Direct GUI access over TCP 3389</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. The Triad of High-Confidence Lateral Movement Event IDs</h2>\n<p>During active internal reconnaissance and lateral movement, the following three event IDs form a high-fidelity alert chain:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Event ID</th>\n      <th>Log Channel</th>\n      <th>Forensic Significance</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4672</strong></td>\n      <td>Security</td>\n      <td><strong>Special Privileges Assigned</strong>: Logged when high-tier privileges (e.g. <code>SeDebugPrivilege</code>) are granted to a newly authenticated session</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4720</strong></td>\n      <td>Security</td>\n      <td><strong>A user account was created</strong>: Generated when attackers establish persistence via shadow administrative accounts</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>7045</strong></td>\n      <td>System</td>\n      <td><strong>A new service was installed</strong>: <strong>The golden indicator for PsExec / Metasploit!</strong> Logged whenever remote service execution drops a service binary</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>3. Case Study: Reconstructing a PsExec Lateral Movement Kill Chain</h2>\n<p>When an attacker executes <code>psexec.exe \\\\192.168.1.50 -u admin -p pass cmd.exe</code>, the target host generates a sequential forensic trail:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[1. Source 192.168.1.100 initiates SMB connection]\n       │\n       ▼ Triggers Event ID 4624 (Logon Type 3: Network Logon)\n[2. Authenticates administrative credentials and assigns token privileges]\n       │\n       ▼ Triggers Event ID 4672 (Assigns SeSecurityPrivilege and SeDebugPrivilege)\n[3. Uploads and registers the temporary PSEXESVC service]\n       │\n       ▼ Triggers System Event ID 7045 (ServiceName: PSEXESVC, ImagePath: C:\\Windows\\PSEXESVC.exe)\n[4. Attacker receives interactive SYSTEM command shell!]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Automated Forensic Extraction with PowerShell</h2>\n<p>Query Windows Event logs using the native <code>Get-WinEvent</code> cmdlet to parse remote logins within seconds:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-powershell\"># Extract all RDP (Logon Type 10) logins from the last 24 hours\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{\n    LogName = 'Security'\n    Id = 4624\n    StartTime = (Get-Date).AddDays(-1)\n} | Where-Object {\n    $_.Properties[8].Value -eq 10\n} | Select-Object TimeCreated, \n    @{N='User'; E={$_.Properties[5].Value}}, \n    @{N='Source_IP'; E={$_.Properties[18].Value}},\n    @{N='LogonType'; E={$_.Properties[8].Value}} | Format-Table -AutoSize</code></pre>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The <strong>Logon Type field in Event ID 4624</strong> distinguishes between local physical logins, SMB lateral pivots (Type 3), and RDP sessions (Type 10).</li>\n    <li><strong>System Log Event ID 7045</strong> provides immediate detection for remote service creation tools like PsExec.</li>\n    <li>Deploy <strong>Windows Event Forwarding (WEF)</strong> to an immutable central SIEM to defeat anti-forensic log wiping (<code>wevtutil cl</code>).</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Logon Triage Analysis</div>\n  <p>Review the following log telemetry:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Event ID: 4624\nTargetUserName: Administrator\nLogonType: 3\nIpAddress: 192.168.10.45\nWorkstationName: KALI-ATTACKER</code></pre>\n  <p>Determine whether this event reflects legitimate local workstation activity or an active network lateral movement attempt, citing two technical indicators.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Windows Security Event Log analysis, what logon mode is represented by Event ID 4624 with Logon Type 10?","options":["Interactive direct console physical keyboard authentication (Interactive)","Network share access, such as SMB / IPC$ connections (Network)","Remote Desktop Services session connection (RemoteInteractive / RDP)","Background Windows Service initiation (Service)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logon Type 10 specifically designates a remote interactive logon via Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) or Terminal Services."},{"question":"When auditing lateral movement tools like PsExec or malicious remote service creation, which Windows System Log Event ID is paramount?","options":["Event ID 7045 (A new service was installed in the system)","Event ID 1102 (The audit log was cleared)","Event ID 4634 (An account was logged off)","Event ID 6005 (The Event Log service was started)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tools like PsExec remotely register temporary Windows services (e.g., PSEXESVC). Event ID 7045 records the exact service name, binary image path, and installing user account."}]},
  "dfir-01-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Endpoint Telemetry</div>\n  <h1>Sysmon Deployment &amp; High-Fidelity Threat Hunting</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Default Windows security logs lack granularity; they fail to record complete command-line parameters, outbound socket telemetry, or in-memory injection techniques. Microsoft Sysinternals Sysmon (System Monitor) serves as the industry-standard free EDR agent, bridging this critical visibility gap. This lesson covers Sysmon driver internals and production threat hunting configurations.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The Sysmon Architecture</strong>: Utilizing kernel-mode minifilter drivers to track process execution, network sockets, and memory injection across the OS lifecycle.</li>\n    <li><strong>Four Premier Detection Event IDs</strong>: Event 1 (Process Creation with full CommandLine), Event 3 (Network Connections), Event 8 (CreateRemoteThread Injection), and Event 11 (File Creation).</li>\n    <li><strong>Deploying SwiftOnSecurity Configuration Baselines</strong>: Filtering benign enterprise noise while surgically intercepting obfuscated PowerShell and LOLBin abuse.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Native Windows Visibility Gap</h2>\n<p>While native Event 4688 records process execution, it <strong>omits command-line execution parameters by default</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Default Windows 4688] Reports only: \"powershell.exe was launched\"\n[Attacker's Actual Command] In reality: \"powershell.exe -w hidden -enc JABzACAAPQAgAE4AZQB3AC0ATwBi...\"\n[Result: Without command-line arguments, defenders cannot distinguish administration from in-memory droppers!]</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Sysmon Core Event ID Matrix</h2>\n<p>Sysmon couples a Windows service with a kernel driver, outputting events to <code>Applications and Services Logs/Microsoft/Windows/Sysmon/Operational</code>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Event ID</th>\n      <th>Telemetry Category</th>\n      <th>High-Risk Detection Scenarios</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1</strong></td>\n      <td>Process Creation</td>\n      <td><strong>The foundation of threat hunting!</strong> Records ParentImage, full CommandLine, and file hashes (SHA256/Imphash)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3</strong></td>\n      <td>Network Connection</td>\n      <td>Maps network sockets directly to initiating process binaries, catching reverse shells in real time</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>7</strong></td>\n      <td>Image Loaded</td>\n      <td>Tracks DLL loading events, uncovering DLL search-order hijacking and unsigned kernel drivers</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>8</strong></td>\n      <td>CreateRemoteThread</td>\n      <td><strong>Core process injection indicator!</strong> Flags shellcode injection into legitimate system processes (e.g. <code>svchost.exe</code>)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>11</strong></td>\n      <td>FileCreate</td>\n      <td>Tracks payload drops and staging of secondary ransomware encryptor binaries</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>3. Hardened XML Configuration via SwiftOnSecurity</h2>\n<p>Running Sysmon without filtering generates thousands of events per second, exhausting storage. Production deployments require high-fidelity XML rules:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-xml\">&lt;Sysmon schemaversion=\"4.90\"&gt;\n  &lt;HashAlgorithms&gt;md5,sha256,imphash&lt;/HashAlgorithms&gt;\n  &lt;EventFiltering&gt;\n    &lt;!-- Process Creation Rules (Event ID 1) --&gt;\n    &lt;RuleGroup name=\"ProcessCreation\" groupRelation=\"or\"&gt;\n      &lt;ProcessCreate onmatch=\"include\"&gt;\n        &lt;!-- Detect certutil repurposed as an arbitrary downloader --&gt;\n        &lt;CommandLine condition=\"contains\"&gt;urlcache&lt;/CommandLine&gt;\n        &lt;!-- Flag vssadmin shadow copy purges prior to ransomware encryption --&gt;\n        &lt;CommandLine condition=\"contains\"&gt;delete shadows&lt;/CommandLine&gt;\n        &lt;!-- Catch base64 encoded PowerShell invocations --&gt;\n        &lt;CommandLine condition=\"contains\"&gt;-enc&lt;/CommandLine&gt;\n      &lt;/ProcessCreate&gt;\n    &lt;/RuleGroup&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- Process Injection Rules (Event ID 8) --&gt;\n    &lt;RuleGroup name=\"ProcessInjection\" groupRelation=\"or\"&gt;\n      &lt;CreateRemoteThread onmatch=\"include\"&gt;\n        &lt;!-- Prohibit non-system processes from injecting threads into lsass.exe --&gt;\n        &lt;TargetImage condition=\"is\"&gt;C:\\Windows\\System32\\lsass.exe&lt;/TargetImage&gt;\n      &lt;/CreateRemoteThread&gt;\n    &lt;/RuleGroup&gt;\n  &lt;/EventFiltering&gt;\n&lt;/Sysmon&gt;</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Management &amp; Hot-Reload Commands</h2>\n<p>Deploy and update Sysmon without host reboots:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-cmd\">:: Install Sysmon and apply initial XML configuration\nSysmon64.exe -i sysmonconfig-export.xml -accepteula\n\n:: Hot-reload configuration changes dynamically\nSysmon64.exe -c updated_rules.xml\n\n:: Query current driver and filtering schema status\nSysmon64.exe -s</code></pre>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Sysmon bridges the gap in Windows event logging by providing <strong>complete command-line arguments, parent-child process lineages, and injection telemetry</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Event ID 1 (CommandLine parameters) and Event ID 8 (CreateRemoteThread)</strong> are critical for uncovering evasive living-off-the-land attacks.</li>\n    <li>Deploying validated rule templates like <strong>SwiftOnSecurity</strong> eliminates noise while spotlighting high-risk indicators.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Analyzing a Process Injection Alert</div>\n  <p>An analyst observes the following Sysmon Event ID 8 alert:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><code>SourceImage: C:\\Users\\Public\\word_preview.exe</code></li>\n    <li><code>TargetImage: C:\\Windows\\System32\\svchost.exe</code></li>\n    <li><code>StartAddress: 0x00007FFA34100000</code></li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Does this reflect normal system execution or an active process injection attack? Why did the adversary target <code>svchost.exe</code>?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What unique telemetry capability makes Sysmon indispensable compared to default Windows native event logging?","options":["Sysmon automatically functions as an antivirus engine to delete malicious files","Sysmon captures complete process execution command lines (CommandLine), parent process GUIDs, and cryptographic image hashes (Event ID 1)","Sysmon optimizes network interface throughput and minimizes latency","Sysmon patches all operating system 0-day kernel flaws automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"While native Event ID 4688 requires optional auditing policies to capture arguments, Sysmon Event ID 1 captures the full command-line arguments, parent-child process tree, and binary hashes out of the box."},{"question":"In Sysmon threat telemetry, what malicious activity is directly indicated by Event ID 8 (CreateRemoteThread)?","options":["Connecting an unauthorized USB flash storage device","A high-volume file transfer downloaded from the external internet","Cross-process memory injection (e.g., injecting payload shellcode into svchost.exe or lsass.exe)","Installation of a new system font file"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CreateRemoteThread allows one process to spawn an execution thread inside the virtual memory space of another target process—a hallmark technique of covert in-memory malware."}]},
  "dfir-02-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Incident Models</div>\n  <h1>NIST/SANS IR Lifecycle &amp; The Golden 4-Hour SOP</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">\"There is no impenetrable perimeter; true resilience lies in rapid containment and damage limitation.\" When an intrusion erupts, panic is often more damaging than the adversary. This lesson teaches the NIST/SANS Incident Response (PICERL) framework and operationalizes the \"Golden Four-Hour\" triage SOP.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The 6 Phases of PICERL</strong>: Preparation, Identification, Containment, Eradication, Recovery, and Lessons Learned.</li>\n    <li><strong>RFC 3227 Order of Volatility</strong>: Never pull the power plug! Adhering to the scientific hierarchy from CPU registers and RAM down to disk archives.</li>\n    <li><strong>The Golden 4-Hour Triage SOP</strong>: Operational containment, credential revocation, and communication playbooks.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The PICERL Incident Response Lifecycle</h2>\n<p>When enterprise systems suffer unauthorized compromise, the standardized PICERL lifecycle ensures structured operational execution:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[1. Preparation] ──Hardening baselines, deploying EDR, immutable cold backups\n        │\n        ▼\n[2. Identification] ──Validating breach scope, identifying patient zero, triage\n        │\n        ▼\n[3. Containment] ──Network isolation, killing egress, protecting core assets (Golden 4-Hours!)\n        │\n        ▼\n[4. Eradication] ──Purging implants, revoking stolen credentials, patching exploits\n        │\n        ▼\n[5. Recovery] ──Validating integrity, restoring clean backups, rigorous monitoring\n        │\n        ▼\n[6. Lessons Learned] ──Root cause analysis, engineering new detections, post-mortem</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Evidence Preservation: RFC 3227 Order of Volatility</h2>\n<p>Untrained administrators frequently default to <strong>\"pulling the physical power cord\"</strong>. In digital forensics, this represents a catastrophic blunder:</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-danger\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">🚨 Absolute Directive: Never Pull the Physical Power Plug!</div>\n  <p>Abruptly severing power wipes physical RAM in milliseconds. In-memory shellcode, active socket connections, decryption keys, and uncommitted log buffers evaporate instantly, causing permanent loss of critical legal and forensic evidence.</p>\n</div>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Volatility Hierarchy</th>\n      <th>Storage Media Category</th>\n      <th>Forensic Value &amp; Acquisition Vectors</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1 (Most Volatile)</strong></td>\n      <td>CPU Registers &amp; L1/L2 Cache</td>\n      <td>Active instruction pointers, plaintext encryption keys (hardware acquisition)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2</strong></td>\n      <td>Physical RAM</td>\n      <td><strong>Core Forensic Target!</strong> Fileless droppers, active sockets, injected shellcode</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3</strong></td>\n      <td>Network State &amp; Routing Tables</td>\n      <td>Active sockets (<code>netstat -ano</code>), ARP caches, routing configurations</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4</strong></td>\n      <td>Local Storage Disks</td>\n      <td>Registry hives, event logs (EVTX), file system MFT tables (persists post-reboot)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>5 (Least Volatile)</strong></td>\n      <td>Offsite Backups &amp; Archives</td>\n      <td>Historical database backups, offsite SIEM telemetry stores</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>3. The Golden 4-Hour Triage SOP</h2>\n<p>From the minute an intrusion is confirmed, the clock starts ticking on the Golden 4-Hour playbook:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Minutes 0-30: Isolation &amp; Hemostasis</strong>:\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Isolate without powering down</strong>: Sever host network access via EDR quarantine or switch VLAN segmentation, keeping the host alive.</li>\n      <li>Avoid running intrusive third-party cleaners that overwrite volatile memory pages.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Minutes 30-90: Memory Acquisition &amp; Volatile Triage</strong>:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Acquire full physical memory dumps using trusted tools (DumpIt, WinPmem) executed from a write-blocked USB drive.</li>\n      <li>Capture live socket lists, process trees, and logged-in user tokens.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Minutes 90-180: Scope Assessment &amp; Credential Fusing</strong>:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Determine whether patient zero has administrative ties to Active Directory domain controllers.</li>\n      <li>Trigger enterprise-wide credential rotations for high-privilege service accounts (Domain Admins, krbtgt).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Minutes 180-240: Root Cause Identification &amp; Executive Reporting</strong>:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Isolate initial access vectors (VPN credential stuffing, web exploitation, macro phishing).</li>\n      <li>Publish the initial incident response executive brief for leadership and compliance teams.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Incident triage must strictly adhere to the <strong>PICERL 6-phase framework</strong> to prevent operational missteps.</li>\n    <li>Comply with the <strong>RFC 3227 Order of Volatility</strong>: Isolate hosts from the network while preserving live RAM.</li>\n    <li>The mission of the \"Golden 4-Hours\" is <strong>containment and forensic evidence preservation</strong> rather than premature file deletion.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Incident Decision Triage</div>\n  <p>Scenario: Friday 22:00, SOC alarms flag a core database server compromised by cryptomining malware at 100% CPU. Which operational workflow is correct?</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Workflow A: Reboot the host ➔ Install consumer antivirus ➔ Perform a full scan.</li>\n    <li>Workflow B: Logically isolate outbound internet routing while preserving internal read queries ➔ Acquire a raw RAM dump ➔ Inspect the mining PID and socket parameters ➔ Remediate root cause.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"According to the NIST SP 800-61 incident response lifecycle, what is the correct sequence of the four foundational phases?","options":["Containment & Eradication -> Preparation -> Post-Incident -> Detection & Analysis","Preparation -> Detection & Analysis -> Containment, Eradication & Recovery -> Post-Incident Activity","Detection & Analysis -> Post-Incident -> Preparation -> Containment & Eradication","Preparation -> Operating System Reinstallation -> Detection & Analysis -> Legal Prosecution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The NIST SP 800-61 standard designates: 1. Preparation -> 2. Detection & Analysis -> 3. Containment, Eradication & Recovery -> 4. Post-Incident Activity."},{"question":"During the critical 'Golden 4-Hour' containment window of a live enterprise compromise, what is the blue team's highest-priority tactical action?","options":["Immediately rebooting all compromised systems to flush volatile RAM","Manually deleting adversary scripts on live network connections without isolation","Enforcing swift network containment to halt lateral propagation and exfiltration while preserving volatile memory","Publishing an immediate public press disclosure acknowledging breach status"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The initial 4-hour window prioritizes containment to sever adversary control channels and prevent lateral blast radius expansion while securing volatile forensic evidence."}]},
  "dfir-02-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Ransomware Containment</div>\n  <h1>Ransomware Triage &amp; Kill-Chain Interruption</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Ransomware attacks represent an existential threat to modern enterprises. The transition from initial foothold to full-scale domain encryption frequently transpires in under an hour. When ransom notices populate desktop monitors, how do responders halt the encryption loop, preserve unencrypted volumes, and profile the threat syndicate? This lesson covers emergency ransomware containment SOPs.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The 4-Step Emergency Containment Playbook</strong>: Physical network disconnection, process suspension (not killing!), blocking lateral ports (445/139/3389), and salvaging Volume Shadow Copies.</li>\n    <li><strong>Profiling Ransomware Signatures</strong>: Correlating file extension patterns, ransom note naming conventions, and Tor negotiation endpoints.</li>\n    <li><strong>Forensic Key Recovery Possibilities</strong>: Extracting transient symmetric keys from process memory dumps and leveraging public decryption repositories (No More Ransom).</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Emergency Containment Checklist</h2>\n<p>When an encryption routine is actively traversing files, seconds dictate the survival of core databases:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>Emergency Ransomware Containment Checklist</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code>[Step 1: Sever Network Connectivity Immediately]\nUnplug physical ethernet or disconnect virtual NICs to stop lateral spreading! Do NOT power down!\n\n[Step 2: Suspend the Process — Never Kill Immediately!]\nWhy Suspend rather than Kill?\nKilling the process immediately reclaims process memory!\nPlaintext symmetric keys (AES/ChaCha20) resident in RAM are destroyed forever!\nCorrect action: Freeze process threads via PsSuspend or Process Hacker!\ncmd# pssuspend.exe evil_ransomware.exe\n\n[Step 3: Audit and Preserve Volume Shadow Copies]\nAttackers script vssadmin delete shadows, but if elevation fails,\nshadow snapshots may survive intact!\ncmd# vssadmin list shadows\n\n[Step 4: Block Lateral Movement Ports on Network Switches]\nImmediately block ports 445 (SMB), 139 (NetBIOS), and 3389 (RDP) cluster-wide!</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Ransomware Family Fingerprinting Matrix</h2>\n<p>Identifying whether an intrusion originates from <strong>LockBit, BlackCat, or legacy variants</strong> guides recovery decisions:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Ransomware Family</th>\n      <th>Encrypted Extension Pattern</th>\n      <th>Ransom Note Filename</th>\n      <th>Technical Characteristics &amp; Decryptability</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>WannaCry</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.WNCRY</code></td>\n      <td><code>@Please_Read_Me@.txt</code></td>\n      <td>MS17-010 wormable; early Windows XP/7 memory tools (Wanakiwi) extracted primes before reboot</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>LockBit 3.0 (Black)</strong></td>\n      <td>Random 9 chars or <code>.HLJkNskOq</code></td>\n      <td><code>[ID].README.txt</code></td>\n      <td>Intermittent multithreaded encryption; mathematically unbreakable without private keys</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>BlackCat (ALPHV)</strong></td>\n      <td>Randomized per victim</td>\n      <td><code>RECOVER-[ID]-NOTES.txt</code></td>\n      <td>Rust-based, targets Windows, Linux, and VMware ESXi hypervisors</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Phobos / Dharma</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.[id].[email].phobos</code></td>\n      <td><code>info.txt</code>, <code>info.hta</code></td>\n      <td>Deployed via RDP brute-forcing; robust key encapsulation</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 Free Decryption Resources: The No More Ransom Project</div>\n  <p>Always cross-reference indicators with <strong>No More Ransom (nomoreransom.org)</strong> and ID Ransomware. Decryption keys seized by global law enforcement (e.g. GandCrab, REvil variants) are frequently released as free public utilities.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Live Process Memory Dumping</h2>\n<p>Prior to terminating suspended ransomware tasks, acquire a full memory dump using <strong>ProcDump</strong>:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-cmd\">:: Dump full process memory space of the suspended encryptor\nprocdump.exe -ma &lt;Ransomware_PID&gt; C:\\IR_Evidence\\ransom_memory.dmp</code></pre>\n<p>Analysts inspect this memory dump using hex editors and Volatility to search for unpurged symmetric key buffers or predictable pseudo-random number generator (PRNG) seeds.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The ransomware triage doctrine: <strong>\"Disconnect network, suspend process, check shadow copies, block lateral ports\"</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Prioritize <strong>suspending the encryptor process</strong> over killing it to preserve symmetric key structures in RAM.</li>\n    <li>Leverage <strong>ID Ransomware and No More Ransom</strong> to query seized master key databases before considering negotiation.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Ransom Note Intelligence Triage</div>\n  <p>A victim desktop exhibits a ransom note named <code>README_FOR_DECRYPT.txt</code> linking to an <code>http://lockbitapt...onion</code> darknet portal. Answer within 30 seconds:</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Which Ransomware-as-a-Service (RaaS) syndicate is responsible?</li>\n    <li>Does this syndicate practice double extortion? What immediate steps should defenders take to assess potential data exfiltration?</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When active ransomware is discovered encrypting storage volumes, why do responders recommend suspending the process rather than immediately terminating it?","options":["Suspending the process causes the ransomware to automatically reverse its encryption","Killing the process can corrupt files currently being written, and suspending retains in-memory cryptographic keys for forensic extraction","Windows operating systems prohibit killing processes with unassigned privileges","Suspending automatically transmits deceptive payment verification tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sudden termination can irreparably corrupt partially written files. Process suspension stops active encryption I/O immediately while preserving ephemeral AES/RSA key schedules in memory for recovery."},{"question":"The command `vssadmin delete shadows /all /quiet`, routinely executed by ransomware prior to encryption, aligns with which MITRE ATT&CK technique?","options":["Initial Access","Privilege Escalation","Impact: Inhibit System Recovery (T1490)","OS Credential Dumping"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Purging Volume Shadow Copies prevents victims from restoring files from local shadow copies, mapping directly to technique T1490: Inhibit System Recovery."}]},
  "dfir-02-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Linux Triage</div>\n  <h1>Linux Live Response: Backdoors, Cron &amp; Process Triage</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Linux hosts the vast majority of enterprise production web infrastructure, cloud containers, and database tiers. When a Linux host exhibits 100% CPU saturation or connects to unauthorized mining pools, incident responders must navigate strictly via CLI to dissect hidden implants. This lesson covers the standardized Linux live response triage manual.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Auditing Users &amp; Privilege Escalations</strong>: Inspecting <code>/etc/passwd</code> for unauthorized UID 0 accounts and auditing <code>/etc/sudoers</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Scanning Persistence Mechanisms</strong>: Uncovering rogue entries across user crontabs, <code>/etc/cron.*</code>, systemd units, and <code>rc.local</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Tracking Deleted Binaries &amp; LD_PRELOAD Rootkits</strong>: Dissecting <code>/proc/[PID]/exe</code> symlinks and neutralizing user-space preloading rootkits.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. User Account &amp; Credential Triage</h2>\n<p>Adversaries establish persistence by spawning shadow administrative accounts or appending rogue SSH keys:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># 1. Audit all non-root accounts possessing UID 0 (root equivalent)!\n$ awk -F: '($3 == 0) {print $1}' /etc/passwd\n\n# 2. List all accounts configured with valid interactive login shells\n$ grep -E '/bin/bash|/bin/sh' /etc/passwd\n\n# 3. Inspect active user sessions and historic login IP origins\n$ who\n$ w\n$ last -n 20 -a\n\n# 4. Audit SSH authorized_keys across root and user directories\n$ cat /root/.ssh/authorized_keys\n$ cat /home/*/.ssh/authorized_keys</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Scheduled Tasks &amp; Persistence Auditing</h2>\n<p>Cryptominers maintain survival via multi-layered cron hooks:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Persistence Audit Commands</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Inspect crontabs across all system user accounts\n$ crontab -l\n$ for u in $(cut -f1 -d: /etc/passwd); do crontab -u $u -l 2&gt;/dev/null; done\n\n# 2. Audit system-wide scheduled task directories\n$ ls -al /etc/cron* /etc/crontab /var/spool/cron/crontabs/\n\n# 3. Identify recently created or modified systemd service unit files\n$ ls -lt /etc/systemd/system/ | head -n 15\n\n# 4. Inspect legacy initialization scripts\n$ cat /etc/rc.local\n$ cat /etc/init.d/*</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Correlating Network Sockets with Processes</h2>\n<p>Trace suspicious egress connections back to originating binaries on disk:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># 1. Display active sockets, foreign IP endpoints, and associated PIDs\n$ netstat -antp | grep ESTABLISHED\n# Or modern socket statistics\n$ ss -tulpn\n\n# 2. Trace the true absolute filesystem path of the running executable\n$ ls -l /proc/&lt;PID&gt;/exe\n# Typical miner output showing unlinked file on disk:\n# lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 0 Mar 15 10:00 /proc/1234/exe -&gt; /tmp/.x/kdevtmpfsi (deleted)\n\n# 3. Extract the full invocation arguments and environment variables\n$ cat /proc/&lt;PID&gt;/cmdline | tr '\\0' ' '\n$ cat /proc/&lt;PID&gt;/environ | tr '\\0' '\\n'</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Advanced Rootkit Triage: /etc/ld.so.preload</h2>\n<p>When <code>top</code> and <code>ps</code> fail to show malicious processes despite pegged CPU fans, suspect a <strong>user-space rootkit via LD_PRELOAD</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Adversaries compile a shared object (e.g. <code>libhide.so</code>) and register it in <code>/etc/ld.so.preload</code>.</li>\n  <li>The hook intercepts <code>readdir()</code> calls from <code>ps</code> and <code>top</code>, hiding specific process IDs from system diagnostics!</li>\n</ul>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Inspect dynamic linker preload configuration (normally empty or absent)\n$ cat /etc/ld.so.preload\n# If a suspicious library is discovered, clear the file immediately to restore visibility!\n$ echo \"\" &gt; /etc/ld.so.preload</code></pre>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The four pillars of Linux live response: <strong>Accounts, Scheduled Tasks, Network Sockets, and <code>/proc/</code> virtual structures</strong>.</li>\n    <li><code>/proc/[PID]/exe</code> unmasks binaries even after attackers execute <code>rm -f</code> on the original disk file.</li>\n    <li>Diagnose hidden processes by inspecting <strong><code>/etc/ld.so.preload</code> user-space rootkits</strong>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Carving Deleted Binaries</div>\n  <p>An attacker deleted <code>/tmp/miner</code> immediately after execution:</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>How can <code>lsof</code> locate this unlinked process?</li>\n    <li>How can responders copy the live binary from <code>/proc/[PID]/exe</code> back to disk to preserve evidence for reverse engineering?</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"During Linux incident response, if an adversary executes `rm -f evil_trojan` to delete a running malware executable from disk, how can investigators recover the intact binary?","options":["By extracting file hashes from `/etc/passwd` and redownloading it","By copying the memory image descriptor directly via `cp /proc/<PID>/exe /tmp/recovered_malware`","By rebooting the server to find the file automatically restored in the trash bin","By reviewing `dmesg` ring buffers to retype hexadecimal opcodes manually"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under Linux, unlinking a file does not purge it if an active process maintains an open file handle. `/proc/<PID>/exe` provides a direct symlink to the executing ELF image in memory."},{"question":"When auditing covert Linux persistence mechanisms beyond standard crontabs, which file can hijack system-wide dynamic linking across all executed binaries?","options":["/etc/resolv.conf","/etc/ld.so.preload","/etc/hosts","/var/log/syslog"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`/etc/ld.so.preload` specifies shared libraries that the dynamic linker must inject into every executed binary, frequently weaponized by user-mode rootkits to hook libc functions."}]},
  "dfir-03-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Host Artifacts</div>\n  <h1>Execution Artifacts: Prefetch, Shimcache &amp; Amcache</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Adversaries routinely execute <code>del /f /q evil.exe</code> to cover their tracks. However, Windows performance-optimization and compatibility subsystems function as an unalterable operational flight recorder, capturing execution timestamps, loaded dependencies, and cryptographic hashes. This lesson deconstructs Prefetch, Shimcache, and Amcache forensics.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Deciphering Prefetch (.pf) Architecture</strong>: Parsing <code>C:\\Windows\\Prefetch</code> to extract run counts, the last 8 execution timestamps, and loaded DLL libraries.</li>\n    <li><strong>Analyzing Application Compatibility Cache (Shimcache)</strong>: Extracting filesystem paths and modification timestamps from the SYSTEM registry hive.</li>\n    <li><strong>Amcache.hve Forensics</strong>: Recovering SHA1 binary hashes, compilation timestamps, and original installation paths for deleted malware.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Windows Prefetch: The Incontestable Execution Black Box</h2>\n<p>To optimize application startup times, Windows monitors the first 10 seconds of process execution, saving memory mapping telemetry under <code>C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\&lt;NAME&gt;-&lt;HASH&gt;.pf</code>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Windows Operating System</th>\n      <th>Execution Timestamps Retained</th>\n      <th>Core Forensic Intelligence</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Windows XP / 7</strong></td>\n      <td>1 timestamp</td>\n      <td>Validates historical execution and tracks total Run Count</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Windows 8 / 10 / 11</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>8 consecutive timestamps</strong></td>\n      <td>Maps multi-day lateral movement timelines even after the executable is purged</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>PECmd — Prefetch Forensic Parsing</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># Parse target Prefetch artifact using Eric Zimmerman's PECmd\n$ PECmd.exe -f \"C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\MIMIKATZ.EXE-B4A15A32.pf\"\n\n[+] Executable Name: MIMIKATZ.EXE\n[+] Run count: 3\n[+] Last run: 2026-03-15 10:12:45 UTC\n[+] Previous run 2: 2026-03-15 09:30:10 UTC\n[+] Previous run 3: 2026-03-14 22:15:00 UTC\n[+] Loaded Files &amp; DLLs:\n    \\VOLUME{01d...}\\WINDOWS\\SYSTEM32\\NTDLL.DLL\n    \\VOLUME{01d...}\\USERS\\VICTIM\\DESKTOP\\MIMIKATZ.EXE\n    \\VOLUME{01d...}\\WINDOWS\\SYSTEM32\\SECUR32.DLL</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Application Compatibility Cache (Shimcache)</h2>\n<p>Shimcache tracks system executables to identify application compatibility issues, storing metadata in kernel memory and committing to the SYSTEM hive upon shutdown:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\AppCompatCache\\AppCompatCache</code></pre>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Attribute 1</strong>: Records file traversal even if an executable was merely browsed via Explorer without manual launching.</li>\n  <li><strong>Attribute 2</strong>: Records the full absolute path and standard Last Modified timestamp.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>3. Amcache.hve: Recovering Cryptographic Hashes</h2>\n<p>When an attacker deletes a dropped implant, how do responders acquire its cryptographic hash for intelligence correlation? <strong>Amcache.hve</strong> (located at <code>C:\\Windows\\appcompat\\Programs\\Amcache.hve</code>):</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Amcache.hve Forensic Telemetry]\n  ├── 1. Plaintext SHA1 Hash of the binary (Ready for VirusTotal matching!)\n  ├── 2. PE Header Compilation Timestamp\n  ├── 3. Original Absolute Path &amp; Volume GUID\n  └── 4. File byte size and metadata version strings</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Comparative Execution Artifacts Matrix</h2>\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Forensic Source</th>\n      <th>Physical Disk Location</th>\n      <th>Core Artifact Output</th>\n      <th>Proves Active Execution?</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Prefetch</strong></td>\n      <td><code>C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\*.pf</code></td>\n      <td>Run count, last 8 execution timestamps, loaded DLLs</td>\n      <td><strong>Definitive (100% Verified Execution)</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Shimcache</strong></td>\n      <td>Registry <code>SYSTEM</code> Hive</td>\n      <td>Full path, Last Modified Time</td>\n      <td>Proves existence; does not guarantee execution</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Amcache</strong></td>\n      <td><code>C:\\Windows\\appcompat\\Programs\\Amcache.hve</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Binary SHA1 Hash</strong>, install/exec path</td>\n      <td><strong>Definitive (100% Execution or Installation)</strong></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>5. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>File deletion does not erase forensic history: <strong>Prefetch, Shimcache, and Amcache form the core execution triad</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Prefetch records up to 8 historical execution timestamps</strong>, exposing multi-stage compromise timelines.</li>\n    <li><strong>Amcache.hve preserves the raw SHA1 hash of deleted executables</strong>, unlocking instant threat intelligence correlation.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Deleted Binary Recovery</div>\n  <p>A malware sample was deleted and the recycle bin emptied. In what order should an investigator acquire artifacts to extract the <strong>execution count, last run timestamp, and SHA1 hash</strong>?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What definitive evidentiary artifact is provided by Windows Prefetch files (located in C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\*.pf)?","options":["The user's plaintext Windows login password","Irrefutable proof of application execution (binary name, run count, last 8 execution timestamps, and loaded DLLs)","Full raw packet captures from the local network adapter","The hardware motherboard CPU serial numbers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefetch tracks application cold launches to optimize performance. `.pf` files record the executable name, run count, execution timestamps, and volume/DLL dependencies loaded within the first 10 seconds."},{"question":"Regarding Windows Shimcache (AppCompatCache) persistence timing, which statement is accurate?","options":["Shimcache is maintained exclusively in kernel memory and is only committed to the SYSTEM registry hive upon graceful shutdown or reboot","Shimcache commits records to disk within milliseconds of an application launch","Shimcache tracks only 64-bit binaries and ignores 32-bit executables completely","Emptying the Recycle Bin purges corresponding Shimcache metadata"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shimcache metadata resides in memory during normal operations and is only written out to `SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\AppCompatCache` when the system shuts down or reboots."}]},
  "dfir-03-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Persistence Triage</div>\n  <h1>Persistence Forensics: Run Keys, Services, WMI &amp; Tasks</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Upon securing initial access, adversaries establish persistence to ensure persistent access across system reboots. From traditional registry Run keys to Scheduled Tasks and WMI Event Subscriptions, this lesson provides a comprehensive forensic analysis of the four primary Windows persistence vectors.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Auditing Registry Run Keys</strong>: Inspecting Run, RunOnce, and Winlogon <code>Userinit</code> and <code>Shell</code> hijacking.</li>\n    <li><strong>Scheduled Task Forensics</strong>: Parsing raw XML definitions under <code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks</code> and uncovering hidden registry Index evasion.</li>\n    <li><strong>WMI Event Subscriptions</strong>: Triaging the <code>__EventFilter</code>, <code>__EventConsumer</code>, and <code>__FilterToConsumerBinding</code> persistence architecture.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Registry Autostart Locations (ASEPs)</h2>\n<p>The Windows registry contains dozens of Auto-Start Extensibility Points (ASEPs) favored by threat actors:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Registry Hive &amp; Key Path</th>\n      <th>Persistence Trigger Mechanism</th>\n      <th>Triage &amp; Anomaly Indicators</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run</code></td>\n      <td>Executes upon user interactive desktop login</td>\n      <td>Suspicious paths under <code>AppData\\Local\\Temp</code> or obfuscated PowerShell</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run</code></td>\n      <td>Executes globally across all authenticating users</td>\n      <td>Requires administrative rights; inspect unsigned binaries</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon</code></td>\n      <td>Initializes user sessions (<code>Userinit</code>, <code>Shell</code>)</td>\n      <td>Default value is <code>explorer.exe</code>; verify commas indicating appended binaries</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Image File Execution Options</code></td>\n      <td>Image File Execution Options (Debugger Hijacking)</td>\n      <td>Inspect <code>sethc.exe</code> (Sticky Keys) mapped to <code>Debugger = cmd.exe</code>!</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Scheduled Task Forensics</h2>\n<p>Modern adversaries utilize Scheduled Tasks to execute payloads on intervals or system idle states:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-powershell\"># 1. Enumerate non-Microsoft scheduled tasks via PowerShell\nGet-ScheduledTask | Where-Object {$_.TaskPath -notmatch \"Microsoft\"} | Select-Object TaskName, TaskPath, State\n\n# 2. Inspect physical task XML definitions on disk:\n# Directory: C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\\n# Each task is stored as a raw XML file detailing triggers and action binaries</code></pre>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Evasion Vector: Stripping the Task Index Registry Value</div>\n  <p>If an adversary removes the <code>Index</code> value from <code>HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Schedule\\TaskCache\\Tree\\&lt;TaskName&gt;</code>, the task becomes <strong>completely invisible in taskschd.msc and schtasks /query</strong>. Responders must audit physical XML files directly under <code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks</code>.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Fileless Persistence: WMI Event Subscriptions</h2>\n<p><strong>WMI Event Subscriptions</strong> provide fileless persistence by firing scripts when system events occur:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[The WMI Subscription Triad]\n  ├── 1. __EventFilter: Trigger criteria (WQL query detecting events/uptime)\n  ├── 2. __EventConsumer: Execution action (CommandLineEventConsumer running PowerShell)\n  └── 3. __FilterToConsumerBinding: Cryptographically binds filter trigger to consumer action</code></pre>\n\n<p>Audit and remediate active WMI subscriptions:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-powershell\"># Query all registered WMI Event Filters\nGet-CimInstance -Namespace root\\subscription -ClassName __EventFilter\n\n# Query registered command-line consumers\nGet-CimInstance -Namespace root\\subscription -ClassName CommandLineEventConsumer\n\n# Query active bindings\nGet-CimInstance -Namespace root\\subscription -ClassName __FilterToConsumerBinding</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The four primary persistence vectors: <strong>Registry Run keys, Windows Services, Scheduled Tasks, and WMI Subscriptions</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Never rely strictly on GUI schedulers; cross-reference physical XML files under <code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>WMI Event Subscriptions operate in a fileless manner</strong>, making them a primary inspection priority during advanced triage.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Sticky Keys Backdoor Detection</div>\n  <p>Execute this registry query:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-cmd\">reg query \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Image File Execution Options\\sethc.exe\"</code></pre>\n  <p>If the key returns <code>Debugger REG_SZ C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe</code>, what backdoor does this indicate? How can an attacker abuse this from the lock screen?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In fileless persistence tradecraft using Windows Management Instrumentation (WMI), which three core WMI class objects are required to establish a trigger?","options":["__SystemEvent, __LogRecord, and __TaskScheduler","__EventFilter (trigger condition), __EventConsumer (action payload), and __FilterToConsumerBinding (relational binding)","Win32_Process, Win32_Service, and Win32_BIOS","CIM_ComputerSystem, CIM_OperatingSystem, and CIM_Directory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The WMI persistence triad requires an `__EventFilter` to detect trigger criteria (e.g. system uptime), an `__EventConsumer` to specify the execution action, and a `__FilterToConsumerBinding` to link them."},{"question":"Which registry path is most frequently exploited by adversaries to establish user-level persistence without requiring local administrator privileges?","options":["HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services","HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run","HKCR\\CLSID\\{645FF040-5081-101B-9F08-00AA002F954E}","HKCU\\Control Panel\\Desktop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run` executes automatically when the specific user logs in and can be modified without elevated administrative rights."}]},
  "dfir-03-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">User Telemetry</div>\n  <h1>User Activity Forensics: Shellbags, Jump Lists &amp; LNK Files</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">When adversaries access a compromised workstation, which confidential folders did they explore? Which design documents were exfiltrated via external USB media? Even if files are shredded and history caches cleared, Windows Explorer architecture preserves these actions. This lesson explores Shellbags, Jump Lists, LNK files, and Recycle Bin forensics.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>LNK Files &amp; Jump Lists Forensics</strong>: Reconstructing accessed file paths, MACB timestamps, drive serial numbers, and network shares.</li>\n    <li><strong>Shellbags Folder Browsing Reconstruction</strong>: Proving folder exploration history even after directories are deleted or USB drives detached.</li>\n    <li><strong>Recycle Bin ($Recycle.Bin) Architecture</strong>: Pairing <code>$I</code> metadata index structures with <code>$R</code> data files to prove intentional destruction.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. LNK Files and Jump Lists</h2>\n<p>Windows quietly generates <code>.lnk</code> shortcut files under <code>%APPDATA%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Recent\\</code> whenever a user opens documents:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Forensic Artifact</th>\n      <th>Filesystem Path</th>\n      <th>Evidentiary Intelligence</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>LNK Shortcuts</strong></td>\n      <td><code>%APPDATA%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Recent\\*.lnk</code></td>\n      <td>Original absolute path, creation/modification timestamps, storage volume serial number, target MAC address</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Jump Lists</strong></td>\n      <td><code>%APPDATA%\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Recent\\AutomaticDestinations\\</code></td>\n      <td>Application-specific recent and pinned files (e.g. Word, Chrome, Notepad activity streams)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Folder Traversal Forensics: Shellbags</h2>\n<p>To retain folder window sizing, icon positioning, and view states, Windows commits directory metadata to <strong>UsrClass.dat</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">HKCU\\Software\\Classes\\Local Settings\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\BagMRU\nHKCU\\Software\\Classes\\Local Settings\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\Bags</code></pre>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 The Power of Shellbags Forensics</div>\n  <p>If an attacker connects a flash drive, navigates to <code>E:\\Confidential_Financials_2026</code>, exfiltrates data, and unplugs the drive, no file remains on disk. However, host Shellbags retain an immutable record proving the operator traversed that external directory.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Windows Recycle Bin ($Recycle.Bin) Internals</h2>\n<p>When files are sent to the Recycle Bin, they are moved to <code>C:\\$Recycle.Bin\\&lt;User_SID&gt;\\</code> and split into two companion files:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[User deletes: financial_audit.xlsx]\n          │\n          ▼ (Split into companion files)\n  ├── $Rxxxxxx.xlsx: Complete raw data payload (original file content)\n  └── $Ixxxxxx.xlsx: 544-byte binary index header\n         ├── 1. Original full file path: C:\\Users\\Alice\\Documents\\financial_audit.xlsx\n         ├── 2. Original file size in bytes: 1,048,576\n         └── 3. Millisecond-accurate deletion timestamp: 2026-03-15 10:45:20.123 UTC</code></pre>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>LNK files and Jump Lists</strong> reconstruct documents opened by adversaries and expose external volume serial numbers.</li>\n    <li><strong>Shellbags preserve folder exploration history</strong> even if external storage drives are detached or directories deleted.</li>\n    <li>The Recycle Bin utilizes <strong><code>$I</code> metadata headers and <code>$R</code> data payloads</strong> to reconstruct deleted records and identify user SIDs.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Parsing Recycle Bin Metadata</div>\n  <p>An investigator extracts <code>$IA3BF21.docx</code> from <code>$Recycle.Bin</code>. What 3 core evidentiary data points can be extracted using <code>RBCmd.exe</code>?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Windows forensic artifact analysis, what investigative value is uniquely derived from Shellbags?","options":["Proving that a user actively navigated and browsed specific folder structures via Windows Explorer, even if the folders have been deleted or unmounted","Enumerating total outbound UDP packets dispatched across a network interface","Recovering session authentication cookies cached by third-party web browsers","Modifying motherboard BIOS startup parameters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shellbags preserve user viewing preferences (e.g., icon size, grid view) for directories browsed through Explorer. These records persist even after the folder is deleted or an external drive is detached."},{"question":"When investigating suspect intellectual property exfiltration via removable media, which registry key documents initial USB connection timestamps?","options":["HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Enum\\USBSTOR","HKCU\\Printers\\DevModePerUser","HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows","HKCU\\Environment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Enum\\USBSTOR` stores device descriptors, vendor and product IDs, device serial numbers, and driver enumeration timestamps for attached USB mass storage hardware."}]},
  "dfir-04-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Memory Acquisition</div>\n  <h1>Memory Acquisition: DumpIt, LiME &amp; Chain of Custody</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Physical Random Access Memory (RAM) serves as the digital courtroom of cyber defense forensics. Heavily obfuscated, polymorphic, and packed payloads must expand into raw, unencrypted execution pages within system memory. How do responders extract hundreds of gigabytes of live memory safely? This lesson explores memory acquisition tooling and Chain of Custody compliance.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Live Memory Acquisition Tooling</strong>: Utilizing DumpIt, WinPmem, and FTK Imager across Windows; deploying LiME (Linux Memory Extractor) across kernel trees.</li>\n    <li><strong>Minimizing the Forensic Footprint</strong>: Preventing page overwrites by staging tools and dumping memory strictly over external, write-blocked media or network sockets.</li>\n    <li><strong>Establishing the Judicial Chain of Custody</strong>: Calculating immediate SHA-256 hash digests to guarantee evidence tamper-resistance in legal proceedings.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Principles of Memory Acquisition: Minimizing the Footprint</h2>\n<p>Running any executable on a compromised host inevitably alters memory page tables. To minimize volatile evidence corruption, follow the <strong>principle of the least forensic footprint</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Tool Staging</strong>: Never copy acquisition binaries to the target machine's local hard drive! Execute tools directly from write-blocked external storage or an isolated SMB read-only mount.</li>\n  <li><strong>Target Dump Destination</strong>: A memory image matches physical RAM size (e.g. 32GB, 128GB). Stream dump output directly to external high-speed storage or over a raw Netcat socket.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Platform</th>\n      <th>Recommended Utility</th>\n      <th>Architecture &amp; Considerations</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Windows</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>DumpIt / WinPmem</strong></td>\n      <td>Lightweight CLI tools loading transient kernel drivers to read <code>\\Device\\PhysicalMemory</code> with minimal footprint</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Windows GUI</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>FTK Imager Lite</strong></td>\n      <td>Portable GUI utility capable of acquiring RAM, pagefile.sys, and protected registry hives simultaneously</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Linux</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>LiME</strong></td>\n      <td>Open-source Loadable Kernel Module (LKM) capable of streaming raw memory directly over TCP sockets</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Virtualization</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Hypervisor Snapshots</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Zero-footprint acquisition!</strong> Capture live guest state directly from ESXi/KVM (<code>.vmem</code> files) with zero host awareness</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Hands-on: Live Windows Memory Acquisition</h2>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>cmd — Memory Acquisition with WinPmem</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Mount write-blocked external storage drive (assigned to E:)\nE:Forensic_Tools&gt; winpmem_mini_x64.exe -o E:Evidence_Store\node01_ram.raw\n\n[+] Extracting driver to temporary storage...\n[+] Initializing memory physical map...\n[+] System has 34359738368 bytes (32.0 GB) of physical memory.\n[+] Progress: 100% [========================================]\n[+] Memory acquisition completed successfully in 82 seconds.\n\n# 2. Immediately compute and log the cryptographic SHA-256 evidence hash!\nE:Evidence_Store&gt; certutil -hashfile node01_ram.raw SHA256\nSHA256 hash of node01_ram.raw:\ne3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\nCertUtil: -hashfile command completed successfully.</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Establishing the Chain of Custody</h2>\n<p>In regulatory audits and courtroom prosecutions, defense attorneys scrutinize acquisition methods. The Chain of Custody ensures evidence integrity:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Dual Hashing</strong>: Immediately calculate SHA-256 and MD5 digests upon dump completion, entering both on physical evidence intake forms.</li>\n  <li><strong>Timestamp &amp; Custodian Logs</strong>: Document precise start/finish timestamps, hardware serial numbers, and acquisition engineer credentials.</li>\n  <li><strong>Working on Forensic Clones Only</strong>: <strong>Never conduct analysis directly on the primary raw image!</strong> Create bit-stream verified copies and mount them in read-only forensic workstations.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Memory acquisition adheres to the <strong>principle of least forensic footprint</strong>; store tools and dumps on external write-blocked storage.</li>\n    <li>In virtualized environments, <strong>hypervisor snapshots (.vmem) deliver true zero-footprint memory capture</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Immediately <strong>calculate and document SHA-256 digests</strong> upon acquisition completion to anchor the legal Chain of Custody.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Evidence Verification</div>\n  <p>An investigator captures <code>mem.raw</code> on site with SHA-256 digest <code>aabbcc...</code>. One week later, the lab analyst recalculates the digest as <code>112233...</code>. Identify two operational factors that explain this discrepancy and assess whether the evidence remains admissible in court.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Under RFC 3227 guidelines for Order of Volatility during forensic evidence triage, which data must be gathered first?","options":["Cold optical archival backup disks","CPU registers, cache memory, and volatile physical system RAM","Local printer document spool queues","Historical log archives located on remote Network Attached Storage (NAS)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Data with the shortest operational lifespan must be acquired first. CPU registers and volatile RAM vanish immediately upon power disconnection, taking priority over persistent media."},{"question":"Why should incident responders avoid forcing a workstation into hibernation (`hiberfil.sys`) as an alternative to dedicated memory dumping tools?","options":["Hibernation files are automatically purged by endpoint antivirus programs","Initiating hibernation executes massive kernel disk writes that overwrite active memory structures and risk data compression corruption","Windows operating systems do not support hibernation mechanics","Hibernation triggers electrical short-circuits across motherboard components"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forcing hibernation invokes extensive disk-write operations that alter memory states, potentially trampling ephemeral shellcode and unlinked process structures in active memory."}]},
  "dfir-04-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Memory Analysis</div>\n  <h1>Volatility 3 Internals: PsList, PsScan, Malfind &amp; NetScan</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Acquiring raw memory yields a multi-gigabyte binary blob. Volatility 3, the global open-source standard for memory forensics, parses complex operating system kernel structures (EPROCESS pools, VAD trees). This lesson explores Volatility 3 plugins, kernel DKOM evasion, and process injection detection.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>The Volatility 3 Symbol Table Architecture</strong>: Eliminating legacy Volatility 2 profile configurations with automatic online/offline Windows symbol parsing.</li>\n    <li><strong>Comparing pslist vs psscan</strong>: Contrasting active linked-list traversals (<code>ActiveProcessLinks</code>) against binary pool tag scanning to unmask unlinked (DKOM) rootkit processes.</li>\n    <li><strong>Deploying malfind and netscan</strong>: Pinpointing suspicious VAD execution permissions and reconstructing historic TCP/UDP network connections.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Volatility 3 Architectural Enhancements</h2>\n<p>Volatility 3 eliminates the manual <code>imageinfo</code> profile identification required in legacy versions, leveraging automated Microsoft PDB symbol tables:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Standard Volatility 3 Invocation Pattern\npython3 vol.py -f &lt;path_to_memory_image&gt; &lt;os.plugin&gt;\n\n# Core Windows Investigation Commands\npython3 vol.py -f mem.raw windows.pslist\npython3 vol.py -f mem.raw windows.pstree\npython3 vol.py -f mem.raw windows.netscan\npython3 vol.py -f mem.raw windows.malfind</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Process Triage: pslist vs psscan (Defeating DKOM)</h2>\n<p>Adversaries employ kernel drivers to modify <code>ActiveProcessLinks</code>, unlinking implants so tools like Task Manager cannot see them:</p>\n\n<table class=\"data-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Plugin Name</th>\n      <th>Underlying Kernel Data Structure</th>\n      <th>Forensic Significance</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>windows.pslist</strong></td>\n      <td>Traverses the <code>ActiveProcessLinks</code> double-linked list starting at <code>PsActiveProcessHead</code></td>\n      <td>Reflects standard OS view; fails to display unlinked DKOM rootkit processes</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>windows.psscan</strong></td>\n      <td>Carves physical memory for <code>EPROCESS</code> pool tag signatures</td>\n      <td><strong>Unmasks hidden processes!</strong> Identifies unlinked tasks as long as process structures reside in memory pages</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>windows.pstree</strong></td>\n      <td>Renders parent-child process relationships in an indented tree view</td>\n      <td>Spots anomalous lineage (e.g. <code>word.exe</code> spawning <code>powershell.exe</code>)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>3. Hunting Code Injection: windows.malfind</h2>\n<p>The <strong>malfind</strong> plugin inspects the Virtual Address Descriptor (VAD) tree of every process, flagging memory pages possessing <strong>executable and writable attributes (PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE)</strong> without backing disk files:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>volatility3 — windows.malfind Output</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code>$ python3 vol.py -f memory.raw windows.malfind --pid 1024\n\nPID    Process       Start VPN       End VPN         Tag    Protection\n1024   svchost.exe   0x000001f3...   0x000001f3...   Vad    PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE\n\nHex Dump:\n4d 5a 90 00 03 00 00 00  04 00 00 00 ff ff 00 00  MZ..............\nb8 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  40 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ........@.......\n\nDisassembly:\n0x1f3000:  dec   ebp\n0x1f3001:  pop   edx\n0x1f3002:  nop\n0x1f3003:  add   [eax], al</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<p>Analysis: <code>svchost.exe</code> is a core service binary. Finding an unmapped <strong>PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE</strong> page containing a <strong>MZ header</strong> confirms reflective DLL injection or process hollowing!</p>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Volatility 3's <strong>symbol table framework</strong> provides accurate, multi-threaded memory parsing across Windows and Linux kernels.</li>\n    <li><strong>Cross-referencing pslist and psscan</strong> exposes rootkits utilizing DKOM linked-list unlinking.</li>\n    <li><strong>windows.malfind serves as the primary tool</strong> for discovering unmapped executable memory segments and injected shellcode stubs.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Anomaly Lineage Analysis</div>\n  <p>Review this <code>windows.pstree</code> excerpt:</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-text\">0x...:svchost.exe (PID: 884)\n  __ 0x...:cmd.exe (PID: 3412)\n        __ 0x...:whoami.exe (PID: 4920)</code></pre>\n  <p>Identify the architectural anomaly present in this process tree.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary architectural difference between the Volatility 3 plugins `windows.pslist` and `windows.psscan`?","options":["pslist inspects disk files, whereas psscan parses registry hives","pslist traverses the doubly linked ActiveProcessLinks list and misses unlinked processes; psscan carves memory pools for _EPROCESS tags to uncover hidden rootkits","pslist operates exclusively on 32-bit dumps, while psscan operates on 64-bit dumps","pslist enumerates only terminated processes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kernel rootkits modify `ActiveProcessLinks` (Direct Kernel Object Manipulation) to hide from process lists. `psscan` bypasses linked lists by carving pool allocations for `_EPROCESS` structures."},{"question":"What core heuristic signature does the Volatility 3 `windows.malfind` plugin evaluate to flag injected code?","options":["Processes consuming greater than 1GB of physical memory","Virtual Address Descriptor (VAD) regions marked PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE that lack backing executable disk files","Programs whose parent process is not explorer.exe","Processes containing plaintext non-ASCII characters in memory"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Legitimate binaries rarely allocate regions with simultaneous execute and write permissions. `malfind` isolates unbacked RWX memory pages containing executable opcodes or PE headers."}]},
  "dfir-04-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Payload Extraction</div>\n  <h1>In-Memory Fileless Malware &amp; Shellcode Extraction</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Modern Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) syndicates and red teams operate under fileless paradigms. Threat actors inject Cobalt Strike Beacons, Meterpreter agents, or custom shellcode into legitimate host processes (<code>explorer.exe</code>, <code>lsass.exe</code>, <code>spoolsv.exe</code>), leaving zero traces on disk. This lesson demonstrates carving injected payloads from raw memory dumps to extract adversary C2 infrastructure configurations.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Anatomy of Reflective DLL Injection</strong>: Recognizing unlinked, orphaned VAD segments lacking disk image mappings.</li>\n    <li><strong>Extracting Injected Memory Pages with Volatility 3</strong>: Utilizing <code>windows.dumpfiles</code> and <code>vadyarascan</code> to carve raw memory blocks into discrete <code>.dmp</code> binaries.</li>\n    <li><strong>Reverse Engineering Extracted Payloads</strong>: Deobfuscating shellcode to extract hardcoded C2 domains, sleeping frequencies, and jitter percentages.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Memory Topology of Injected Processes</h2>\n<p>While no malicious file resides on disk, memory structures reveal clear indicators of foreign injection:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Compromised Process: explorer.exe (PID 2100)]\n  ├── .text (Executable code segment mapped from C:\\Windows\\explorer.exe)\n  ├── .data (Global data tables)\n  ├── Legitimate system DLLs (ntdll.dll, kernel32.dll with disk image bindings)\n  └── [Anomalous Private Memory Region: 0x0000021A4B000000]\n         ├── Protection: PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE (W+X)\n         ├── AllocationType: MEM_PRIVATE (Zero disk image backing!)\n         └── Content: Injected Cobalt Strike Beacon reflective loader!</code></pre>\n\n<h2>2. Hands-on: Carving Injected Memory Blocks</h2>\n<p>Once <code>windows.malfind</code> identifies the suspicious PID and starting virtual address (Start VPN), carve that region to disk:</p>\n\n<div class=\"terminal-window\">\n  <div class=\"terminal-bar\">\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot red\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot yellow\"></div>\n    <div class=\"terminal-dot green\"></div>\n    <span>bash — Extracting Injected Memory Region</span>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"terminal-body\">\n    <pre><code># 1. Carve target VAD memory page for PID 2100 at base address 0x0000021a4b000000\n$ python3 vol.py -f memory.raw -o ./extracted_dumps \\\n  windows.dumpfiles --pid 2100 --virtaddr 0x0000021a4b000000\n\n# 2. Inspect the carved raw memory dump\n$ ls -lh ./extracted_dumps/\n-rw-r--r-- 1 analyst analyst 256K Mar 15 11:30 file.0x0000021a4b000000.0x21a4b000.dmp\n\n# 3. View the first 32 bytes of carved binary code\n$ xxd -l 32 ./extracted_dumps/file.0x...dmp\n00000000: 4d5a 4152 5548 89e5 4881 ec00 0000 0048  MZARUH..H......H\n00000010: 8d1d e5ff ffff ff50 68...</code></pre>\n  </div>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Deobfuscating the Injected Beacon Configuration</h2>\n<p>Carved memory blocks can be parsed with specialized configuration tools to recover the adversary's C2 profile:</p>\n\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Run beacon parser against the carved memory dump\n$ python3 parse_beacon_config.py ./extracted_dumps/file.0x...dmp\n\n================== COBALT STRIKE BEACON CONFIG ==================\nBeaconType:          HTTPS\nPort:                443\nSleepTime:           60000 (ms)\nJitter:              20 (%)\nC2Server:            cdn-telemetry.azure-edge.org,/api/v2/sync\nUserAgent:           Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64)...\nPublicKey:           MIGfMA0GCSqGSIb3DQEBAQUAA4GNADCBiQ...\n==================================================================</code></pre>\n<p>The unmapped memory segment reveals the adversary's <strong>active C2 domains, callback URLs, sleep intervals, and TLS configurations</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Chapter Summary</h2>\n<div class=\"callout callout-success\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">📌 Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Fileless malware bypasses disk antivirus by <strong>injecting reflective payloads into legitimate host processes</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>MEM_PRIVATE allocations with PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE permissions</strong> are key indicators of memory-injected modules.</li>\n    <li>Carving injected VAD pages unlocks <strong>full extraction of the adversary's C2 infrastructure, sleep timings, and cryptographic keys</strong>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Shellcode Prologue Analysis</div>\n  <p>Analyze this raw shellcode preamble: <code>FC 48 83 E4 F0 E8 C0 00 00 00 ...</code>. Using <code>scdbg</code> or an emulation engine, determine the purpose of the <code>CLD</code> (0xFC) instruction and subsequent stack alignment operations.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In classical DLL injection, after allocating memory and writing the DLL path into a target process, what Windows API initiates remote execution?","options":["CreateFileA","CreateRemoteThread (or RtlCreateUserThread)","RegOpenKeyExW","GetSystemTime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The classical injection chain executes: OpenProcess -> VirtualAllocEx -> WriteProcessMemory -> CreateRemoteThread pointing to LoadLibraryA as its thread entry point."},{"question":"What defines the core operational mechanism of Process Hollowing?","options":["Uninstalling and removing legitimate software packages from the endpoint","Spawning a legitimate binary in a suspended state (CREATE_SUSPENDED), unmapping its legitimate code section, writing malicious PE payload sections, and resuming the thread","Storing malicious scripts inside the registry for auto-run execution","Flooding network ports with high-volume UDP traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Process Hollowing spawns a benign binary (e.g. svchost.exe), unmaps its memory via `NtUnmapViewOfSection`, writes adversary PE headers/sections, and resumes execution disguised as the benign host."}]},
  "dfir-05-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Traffic Forensics</div>\n  <h1>Wireshark & Tshark Forensics: Payload Reconstruction & Credential Carving</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Full Packet Capture (PCAP) represents the immutable time capsule of digital forensic investigations. When endpoint evidence is tampered with or purged by adversary anti-forensic techniques, mirrored network traffic often remains the single unassailable source of truth. This section delivers a deep dive into Wireshark and the command-line engine Tshark to isolate adversarial sessions, reassemble TCP streams, and carve weaponized payloads and cleartext credentials directly from raw network packets.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Master advanced Wireshark display filters and statistical profiling (Conversations, Protocol Hierarchy).</li>\n    <li>Reconstruct interactive WebShell commands and reverse shells via <strong>TCP Stream Reassembly</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Carve binaries, malicious scripts, and exfiltrated documents from HTTP, SMB, and FTP streams via <strong>File Carving</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Execute high-throughput batch extraction and automated credential hunting across gigabyte-scale PCAPs using <strong>Tshark CLI scripts</strong>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Four-Stage PCAP Investigation Pipeline</h2>\n<p>When analyzing large capture files, scrolling through packets haphazardly is completely ineffective. Investigators adhere to a structured four-stage methodology:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Stage</th>\n      <th>Primary Objective</th>\n      <th>Wireshark Feature & Navigation Path</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Macro Profiling</strong></td>\n      <td>Assess protocol proportions, anomaly volume spikes, and top conversational pairs</td>\n      <td><code>Statistics -> Protocol Hierarchy</code> to spot unauthorized protocols; <code>Statistics -> Conversations</code> to rank highest-volume IP pairs.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Anomaly Slicing</strong></td>\n      <td>Filter out error response codes, non-standard ports, and cleartext services</td>\n      <td>Apply filters such as <code>http.response.code >= 400</code>, <code>tcp.port in {4444, 1337, 8888}</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Stream Reassembly</strong></td>\n      <td>Reconstruct complete bidirectionally ordered communication exchanges</td>\n      <td>Right-click any packet and select <code>Follow -> TCP Stream / HTTP Stream</code> to read raw payload interactions.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Object Carving</strong></td>\n      <td>Isolate executable binaries, dropped scripts, or exfiltrated documents</td>\n      <td>Navigate to <code>File -> Export Objects -> HTTP / SMB</code> to save files to disk and verify cryptographic hashes.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. High-Frequency Display Filter Cheatsheet</h2>\n<p>Display filters evaluate captured packets dynamically with granular protocol-level conditions:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Essential Wireshark Threat Hunting Display Filters</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-plaintext\"># 1. Filter HTTP POST requests (indicative of WebShell commands or exfiltration)\nhttp.request.method == \"POST\"\n\n# 2. Identify file requests matching suspicious executable extensions\nhttp.request.uri matches \"\\.(exe|sh|ps1|php|jsp)$\"\n\n# 3. Detect suspicious or script-driven User-Agents\nhttp.user_agent matches \"(?i)(curl|python|wget|powershell|sqlmap|nikto)\"\n\n# 4. Search for cleartext authentication headers (HTTP Basic, FTP logins)\nhttp.authorization or ftp.request.command == \"USER\" or ftp.request.command == \"PASS\"\n\n# 5. Detect SMB lateral movement and PsExec service pipes\nsmb2.filename contains \"svcctl\" or smb2.filename contains \"psexesvc\"\n\n# 6. Flag TCP SYN port scans (SYN flag set, ACK absent, minimal window size)\ntcp.flags.syn == 1 and tcp.flags.ack == 0 and tcp.window_size <= 1024</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: Carving Malicious Payloads from Network Traffic</h2>\n<p>When an attacker transfers a second-stage dropper over HTTP or drops an implant across SMB admin shares, object extraction reconstructs the file verbatim:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>GUI Extraction</strong>: Select <code>File -> Export Objects -> HTTP...</code>. Sort by <code>Content-Length</code> or <code>Filename</code>, highlight candidates (e.g., <code>beacon.exe</code> or <code>loader.ps1</code>), and click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Raw TCP Stream Carving</strong>: If communication occurs over a raw reverse shell, select <code>Follow -> TCP Stream</code>, switch display format to <code>Raw</code>, and click <code>Save as...</code> to dump the payload byte-for-byte.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>4. Automated High-Throughput Analysis with Tshark</h2>\n<p>During enterprise-grade incident response, PCAP captures frequently exceed tens of gigabytes, easily crashing GUI Wireshark. <strong>Tshark</strong> streams packets through lightweight command-line pipelines:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Pipeline 1: Extract all HTTP request metadata into CSV for hunting</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">tshark -r incident_capture.pcap \\\n  -Y \"http.request\" \\\n  -T fields \\\n  -e frame.time \\\n  -e ip.src \\\n  -e ip.dst \\\n  -e http.host \\\n  -e http.request.uri \\\n  -e http.user_agent \\\n  -E header=y -E separator=',' > http_requests_audit.csv</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Pipeline 2: Frequency analysis of requested DNS hostnames</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">tshark -r incident_capture.pcap \\\n  -Y \"dns.flags.response == 0\" \\\n  -T fields -e dns.qry.name | \\\n  sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 20</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Pipeline 3: Harvest cleartext HTTP Basic credentials</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">tshark -r incident_capture.pcap \\\n  -Y \"http.authorization\" \\\n  -T fields -e ip.src -e http.host -e http.authorization | \\\n  awk '{print $1, $2, $4}' | while read ip host auth; do\n    echo \"[+] Source: $ip -> Host: $host Cred: $(echo $auth | base64 -d 2>/dev/null)\";\n  done</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Common Pitfall</div>\n  <p><strong>Packet Drops and TCP Retransmission Distortion</strong>: Under multi-gigabit mirroring loads, port mirrors or tap interfaces can drop frames, triggering <code>[TCP Previous segment not captured]</code> warnings. If frames are missing, carved binaries may yield corrupted SHA256 hashes. Always verify the capture drop rate under <code>Statistics -> Capture File Properties</code> before drawing definitive forensic conclusions.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Structured Traffic Pipeline</strong>: Advance from protocol hierarchy and conversation tables to targeted display filtering, TCP stream following, and file carving.</li>\n    <li><strong>Tshark for Scale</strong>: Use <code>-Y</code> for filtering and <code>-T fields -e</code> for column projection to process large PCAPs efficiently.</li>\n    <li><strong>Credential Auditing</strong>: Monitor cleartext authentication vectors in HTTP authorization headers, FTP credentials, and unconstrained Kerberos tickets.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Anomaly Evaluation</div>\n  <p>While auditing an internal database server, you discover that every hour on the hour (01:00, 02:00, etc.), the host opens a 3-second TCP connection to an unknown external IP over port 443. However, no TLS handshake occurs, no HTTP headers exist, and the payload consists entirely of high-entropy randomized bytes. What adversarial technique might this indicate, and how would you extract forensic proof?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Wireshark, which display filter specifically isolates HTTP requests commonly leveraged for WebShell interaction and file uploads?","options":["http.response.code == 200","http.request.method == \"POST\"","tcp.port == 8080 and ip.addr == 127.0.0.1","dns.flags.response == 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Interactive WebShell commands and payload drops predominantly utilize HTTP POST requests to transmit instructions and payloads inside the request body."},{"question":"During high-bandwidth traffic capture, what risk is introduced if packet drop occurs on the tap interface?","options":["Wireshark UI font rendering becomes corrupted","Carved executable files may have missing TCP segments, resulting in damaged binaries and inaccurate SHA256 hashes","Network switch ports experience physical hardware burnouts","Target servers automatically trigger emergency shutdowns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TCP stream reassembly requires complete contiguous sequences. Packet loss truncates carved files, invalidating cryptographic hashes and hindering forensic validation."}]},
  "dfir-05-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">C2 Analysis</div>\n  <h1>Covert C2 Communications: DNS Tunneling & HTTP Beacon Jitter Modeling</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Command & Control (C2) communication forms the operational lifeline through which adversaries maintain persistence and control. Modern threat actors leverage covert DNS channels to bypass perimeter firewalls, or inject randomized jitter into HTTP/HTTPS heartbeat beacons to blend seamlessly into benign corporate traffic. This section explores information entropy, temporal distribution analysis, and statistical modeling to uncover covert C2 channels.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Deconstruct <strong>DNS Tunneling mechanics</strong> utilizing Base32/Base64 subdomains and extended TXT records.</li>\n    <li>Understand the mathematical foundation of <strong>Shannon Entropy</strong> for identifying algorithmic subdomains.</li>\n    <li>Analyze the <strong>Heartbeat and Jitter mechanisms</strong> of command frameworks like Cobalt Strike and Sliver.</li>\n    <li>Develop Python temporal frequency modeling scripts to detect C2 beacons across large proxy datasets.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. DNS Covert Channels: Protocol Exploitation & Entropy Analysis</h2>\n<p>In tightly monitored environments, outbound access to non-standard ports is blocked, but internal recursive DNS servers are permitted to query authoritative servers. Attackers deploy authoritative nameservers to route exfiltrated data encoded within subdomains:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Metric</th>\n      <th>Benign Enterprise DNS Baseline</th>\n      <th>DNS Tunneling (dnscat2 / iodine)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Subdomain Length</strong></td>\n      <td>Concise, readable (e.g., <code>api.github.com</code>, &lt; 20 chars)</td>\n      <td>Extended (approaching the RFC limit of 63 characters per label)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Query Record Types</strong></td>\n      <td>Dominated by <code>A</code>, <code>AAAA</code>, and <code>CNAME</code> (&gt; 95%)</td>\n      <td>Substantial volume of <code>TXT</code> (payload transfer) or <code>NULL</code> records</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Resolution Error Rate</strong></td>\n      <td>NXDOMAIN rates typically &lt; 2%</td>\n      <td>Elevated NXDOMAIN / SERVFAIL caused by packet loss and fragmentation</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Shannon Entropy</strong></td>\n      <td>Reflects dictionary words and natural language (&lt; 3.5)</td>\n      <td>High entropy resulting from Base32/Base64 or encrypted strings (&gt; 4.0)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Python Implementation: Subdomain Shannon Entropy Calculator</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import math\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef calculate_shannon_entropy(domain_label: str) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes Shannon Entropy: H(X) = - \\sum P(x) * log2(P(x))\n    Benign labels typically range between 2.0 and 3.5.\n    Encrypted / DGA labels range between 3.8 and 4.8+.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not domain_label:\n        return 0.0\n    length = len(domain_label)\n    freq_map = Counter(domain_label.lower())\n    entropy = -sum((count / length) * math.log2(count / length) for count in freq_map.values())\n    return round(entropy, 4)\n\n# Comparative evaluation:\nbenign = calculate_shannon_entropy(\"mail.corp-portal.company.com\".split(\".\")[0])\nc2_tunnel = calculate_shannon_entropy(\"7b3f9a2c8e1d5a6b0c4e7f8a9b1c2d3e\".split(\".\")[0])\nprint(f\"Benign Subdomain Entropy: {benign}\")       # ~ 1.5 - 2.0\nprint(f\"Tunneling Subdomain Entropy: {c2_tunnel}\") # ~ 4.0 (distinct anomaly)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. HTTP Beacon Heartbeats & Jitter Variance Modeling</h2>\n<p>Implant frameworks transmit periodic beacon signals to retrieve tasks. To evade simple fixed-interval detection, frameworks incorporate <strong>Jitter</strong>:</p>\n<p>Given a baseline sleep interval $T_{sleep} = 60s$ and a jitter factor $Jitter = 20%$, actual connection intervals will be uniformly randomized across $[60 \times (1 - 0.2), 60 \times (1 + 0.2)] = [48s, 72s]$.</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Python Beacon Detection: Identifying Jittered C2 Signals in Proxy Logs</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import numpy as np\n\ndef detect_c2_beacon(timestamps: list[float], max_jitter_tolerance=0.3):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates the Coefficient of Variation (CV = std / mean) of inter-arrival times:\n    Benign human web browsing: CV >> 1.0 (bursty activity followed by idle periods)\n    Strict unjittered C2 (Jitter=0): CV ~ 0.0\n    Jittered C2 (Jitter=20%-50%): CV falls consistently between 0.05 and 0.28\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(timestamps) < 10:\n        return False, \"Insufficient samples\"\n    \n    sorted_ts = sorted(timestamps)\n    deltas = np.diff(sorted_ts)\n    \n    mean_interval = np.mean(deltas)\n    std_interval = np.std(deltas)\n    \n    cv = std_interval / mean_interval if mean_interval > 0 else 999\n    \n    if 0.01 <= cv <= max_jitter_tolerance:\n        return True, {\n            \"Status\": \"HIGH_CONFIDENCE_C2_BEACON\",\n            \"Mean_Sleep_Seconds\": round(mean_interval, 2),\n            \"Interval_StdDev\": round(std_interval, 2),\n            \"CV_Score\": round(cv, 4)\n        }\n    return False, {\"CV_Score\": round(cv, 4)}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. TLS JA3 / JA3S Client Fingerprinting</h2>\n<p>Under full TLS encryption, URL paths and message bodies are encrypted. Defenders distinguish implants from legitimate browsers using <strong>JA3 Fingerprinting</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>JA3 Foundation</strong>: Extracts 5 fields from the TLS Client Hello: <code>SSLVersion,CipherSuites,Extensions,EllipticCurves,EllipticCurvePointFormats</code>, producing an MD5 hash.</li>\n  <li><strong>Anti-Spoofing</strong>: Even if an adversary alters the HTTP User-Agent header to emulate Chrome, the underlying cryptographic stack (e.g., Windows WinINet, Go crypto/tls, or Python requests) generates a distinct Client Hello structure that cannot be easily spoofed.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Client Engine</th>\n      <th>Configured User-Agent</th>\n      <th>JA3 Fingerprint Characteristics</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Google Chrome (Windows)</strong></td>\n      <td>Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64)...</td>\n      <td>Incorporates GREASE extensions and browser-specific cipher order</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Python Requests</strong></td>\n      <td>Can be arbitrarily spoofed to mimic Chrome</td>\n      <td><code>b7423374e1e4cfa12723c623d43d3370</code> (distinct Python signature)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Cobalt Strike Default (WinINet)</strong></td>\n      <td>Commonly configured as Edge or IE</td>\n      <td><code>a0e9f5d64349fb13191bc781f81f42e1</code> (frequent default CS signature)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Common Pitfall</div>\n  <p><strong>Filtering Long Polling and WebSocket False Positives</strong>: Benign applications (messaging platforms, real-time dashboards) utilize regular polling intervals that mimic low-variance beacons. Always cross-correlate temporal scores with <strong>request/response payload sizes</strong> and <strong>domain reputation scores</strong> before classifying traffic as an active intrusion.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>DNS Tunneling Detection</strong>: Shannon entropy &gt; 3.8, elongated subdomains, and recurring TXT/NULL record queries indicate covert DNS encapsulation.</li>\n    <li><strong>Temporal Variance Profiling</strong>: Calculate the coefficient of variation ($CV = sigma / mu$) across communication deltas; a $CV in [0.02, 0.28]$ strongly correlates with jittered C2 beacons.</li>\n    <li><strong>Encrypted Traffic Triangulation</strong>: JA3/JA3S client fingerprinting unmasks underlying communication libraries irrespective of forged User-Agent headers.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Malleable Profile Analysis</div>\n  <p>An internal server initiates repetitive HTTP GET requests to an external endpoint requesting <code>/jquery-3.3.1.min.js?_=&lt;timestamp&gt;</code>. The server returns HTTP 200 with standard jQuery code, but adds an unusual response header: <code>Server: Apache/2.4.41 (Ubuntu)</code>. The requests reoccur approximately every 30 seconds. What evasive C2 strategy does this reflect, and how would you validate whether the server header contains an encoded command payload?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What prominent statistical anomaly characterizes covert DNS tunneling traffic (e.g., iodine, dnscat2)?","options":["Abnormally long subdomains exhibiting high Shannon entropy (Entropy > 3.8) combined with high-frequency TXT/NULL record requests","All DNS queries strictly encapsulated over destination port 443","Subdomains composed strictly of benign English words under 10 characters","Uniform packet sizes locked at precisely 64 bytes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DNS tunneling encodes encrypted chunks into subdomains (producing high Shannon entropy) and frequently leverages TXT/NULL records to transmit maximum payload bytes per query."},{"question":"Why does the implementation of Jitter in C2 beacon frameworks like Cobalt Strike increase defensive detection difficulty?","options":["Jitter encrypts physical layer network cables","Jitter transforms rigid sleep intervals into randomized durations across a bounded range, defeating simple fixed-frequency periodic detections","Jitter automatically signs malware with valid code-signing certificates","Jitter purges access logs on intermediate perimeter firewalls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A 20% jitter on a 60-second sleep disperses callbacks randomly between 48 and 72 seconds, breaking simple periodic correlation and requiring coefficient of variation (CV) variance modeling."}]},
  "dfir-05-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Threat Intel & Attribution</div>\n  <h1>Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) & MITRE ATT&CK Attribution Profiling</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Effective incident response extends beyond removing isolated malicious files; it must answer fundamental questions: \"Who attacked us? How did they enter? What was their objective? Will they return?\". Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) and the MITRE ATT&CK framework provide a common analytical language and strategic operational matrix. This section explores the Pyramid of Pain, the Diamond Model, and actionable threat attribution utilizing STIX/TAXII standards.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Apply David Bianco's <strong>Pyramid of Pain</strong> to prioritize high-leverage defensive engineering over transient IOC matching.</li>\n    <li>Map complex incident killchains onto <strong>MITRE ATT&CK Tactics and Techniques</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Structure adversary relationships using <strong>The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Construct machine-readable threat sharing feeds utilizing the <strong>STIX 2.1 specification</strong>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Pyramid of Pain: Defensive Leverage vs. Adversary Cost</h2>\n<p>Threat intelligence indicators differ dramatically in their operational resilience and adversary replacement overhead:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Layer (Base to Apex)</th>\n      <th>Indicator Category</th>\n      <th>Adversary Replacement Cost</th>\n      <th>Defensive Value & Implementation Strategy</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Hash Values</strong></td>\n      <td>MD5, SHA1, SHA256</td>\n      <td><strong>Trivial</strong>: Modifying a single bit in the binary alters the hash</td>\n      <td>Rapid detection of known files; ineffective for persistent defense.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. IP Addresses</strong></td>\n      <td>IPv4, IPv6 addresses</td>\n      <td><strong>Simple</strong>: Easily cycled via proxies, botnets, or disposable cloud VPS</td>\n      <td>Temporary perimeter blocking; prone to false positives over time.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Domain Names</strong></td>\n      <td>C2 domains, phishing URLs</td>\n      <td><strong>Simple to Moderate</strong>: DGA algorithms, bulletproof registrars</td>\n      <td>DNS sinkholing; monitoring domain age and WHOIS registrations.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Network / Host Artifacts</strong></td>\n      <td>URI regex patterns, registry persistence keys, mutexes</td>\n      <td><strong>Annoying</strong>: Requires code re-architecting and configuration changes</td>\n      <td>Deploy YARA and Snort/Suricata signatures to intercept variants.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>5. Tools</strong></td>\n      <td>Mimikatz, Cobalt Strike, Impacket, BloodHound</td>\n      <td><strong>Challenging</strong>: Forces investment into new software engineering</td>\n      <td>Implement heuristic detection of reflective DLL injection and LSASS access.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>6. TTPs</strong></td>\n      <td>Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (e.g., Pass-the-Hash, DLL Sideloading)</td>\n      <td><strong>Tough</strong>: Demands complete reinvention of operational attack methodology</td>\n      <td>Build MITRE ATT&CK behavioral detections that break entire killchains.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. MITRE ATT&CK Mapping in Live Incident Response</h2>\n<p>The MITRE ATT&CK framework organizes adversary actions across 14 enterprise tactics and hundreds of granular techniques:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Case Study: Ransomware Intrusion Chain Mapped to ATT&CK</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-plaintext\">Phase 1: Initial Access\n   └─ T1566.001 - Phishing: Spearphishing Attachment (Weaponized macro-enabled docm)\n\nPhase 2: Execution\n   └─ T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell (Obfuscated staging script)\n\nPhase 3: Defense Evasion\n   └─ T1027.002 - Obfuscated Files or Information: Software Packing (Reflective loader unpacking)\n   └─ T1070.001 - Indicator Removal: Clear Windows Event Logs (Executed wevtutil cl commands)\n\nPhase 4: Credential Access\n   └─ T1003.001 - OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory (Memory dumped via ProcDump)\n\nPhase 5: Lateral Movement\n   └─ T1021.002 - Remote Services: SMB/Windows Admin Shares (Pushed payload via WMI/SMB)\n\nPhase 6: Impact\n   └─ T1486 - Data Encrypted for Impact (Multi-threaded encryption of network shares)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis</h2>\n<p>Formulated by Sergio Caltagirone, the Diamond Model establishes a formal relational graph for every cyber event:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Adversary</strong>: The threat actor or group behind the campaign (e.g., APT29, FIN7, LockBit Group).</li>\n  <li><strong>Capability</strong>: The tools, exploits, and technical weapons utilized in the operation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Infrastructure</strong>: The network assets, domains, VPS nodes, and command nodes operated by the adversary.</li>\n  <li><strong>Victim</strong>: The targeted enterprise, organization persona, and specific compromised systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By connecting diamonds into an <strong>Activity Thread</strong>, investigators pivot across pivots: discovering an infrastructure node uncovers a linked capability, which shares binary signatures with historical campaigns, revealing the true adversary.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Structured Threat Intelligence Exchange: STIX 2.1</h2>\n<p>STIX 2.1 is the universal JSON-based ontology for standardized CTI representation and sharing:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">STIX 2.1 Bundle: Indicator-to-Malware Relationship Binding</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  \"type\": \"bundle\",\n  \"id\": \"bundle--5d6b4122-1b1a-4c22-b912-706f9d45e112\",\n  \"objects\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"indicator\",\n      \"id\": \"indicator--8e2e2d2b-17d4-4cbf-938f-98ee46b3cd3f\",\n      \"spec_version\": \"2.1\",\n      \"created\": \"2026-09-17T08:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"name\": \"LockBit 3.0 C2 Beacon IP\",\n      \"pattern\": \"[ipv4-addr:value = '198.51.100.45']\",\n      \"pattern_type\": \"stix\",\n      \"valid_from\": \"2026-09-17T08:00:00.000Z\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"malware\",\n      \"id\": \"malware--31b94d13-68ef-4608-8b53-e939abbd3234\",\n      \"spec_version\": \"2.1\",\n      \"created\": \"2026-09-17T08:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"name\": \"LockBit Ransomware\",\n      \"is_family\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"relationship\",\n      \"id\": \"relationship--44c03b12-9c3b-48c9-95e2-e1d09e334a1a\",\n      \"spec_version\": \"2.1\",\n      \"source_ref\": \"indicator--8e2e2d2b-17d4-4cbf-938f-98ee46b3cd3f\",\n      \"target_ref\": \"malware--31b94d13-68ef-4608-8b53-e939abbd3234\",\n      \"relationship_type\": \"indicates\"\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Common Pitfall</div>\n  <p><strong>Guarding Against False Flag Attribution</strong>: Sophisticated APT groups intentionally embed deceptive indicators (e.g., non-native language strings, borrowed staging infrastructure, or copied public tooling) to mislead forensic investigators. Rigorous attribution demands multi-source corroboration across operational timing, geopolitical alignment, and unique codebase lineage rather than superficial strings or single IP addresses.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Ascending the Pyramid of Pain</strong>: Elevate defensive focus from ephemeral hashes and IPs to <strong>TTP behavioral disruption</strong> to impose maximum operational costs on adversaries.</li>\n    <li><strong>ATT&CK for Strategic Modeling</strong>: Standardize the forensic narrative across industry-recognized tactics and techniques to illuminate enterprise visibility gaps.</li>\n    <li><strong>Diamond Model & STIX 2.1</strong>: Model adversary relationships across adversaries, capabilities, infrastructure, and victims, distributing machine-readable intelligence via STIX/TAXII.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: CTI Lifecycle Governance</div>\n  <p>If your SOC ingests hundreds of thousands of open-source IP and file hash indicators directly into perimeter firewalls without curation, causing firewall rule bloat and high false-positive alert volumes, how would you restructure this intelligence pipeline using the Pyramid of Pain and automated indicator aging policies?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In David Bianco's Pyramid of Pain framework, which indicator category resides at the pinnacle, inflicting maximum operational cost on an adversary when countered?","options":["File Hash Values","Adversary IP Addresses","Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs)","Domain Names"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Altering hashes and IP addresses costs adversaries seconds; disrupting fundamental TTPs forces them to re-engineer complete attack lifecycles and offensive capabilities."},{"question":"Under the STIX 2.1 threat intelligence standard, which object formally connects an Indicator object to a specific Malware family?","options":["Campaign","Relationship (using relationship_type: 'indicates')","Identity","Vulnerability"],"answer":1,"explanation":"STIX 2.1 utilizes graph-based Relationship objects specifying `source_ref`, `target_ref`, and `relationship_type: 'indicates'` to link indicators to malware representations."}]}
});
